; 8.3 mm A/V lead (tube_radius 0.00415) — nearly twice the 4.5 mm controller cord
; in controller_cable.tscn, which is right: this is a shielded composite cable.
; collision_radius is the standoff the solver holds each particle at from a contact
; plane, so it has to track the tube or the cord floats; 0.0055 leaves the same
; 1.35 mm resting gap the controller cable uses.
;
; end_stiff_segments counts SEGMENTS, so the boot constraint spans 2 x 30 mm,
; but end_stiffness decides how much of that reads as held. 0.45 tapers it to
; about one segment straight out of the plug, ~30 mm, or 3.6x the 8.3 mm OD —
; a moulded strain relief rather than a rod. Values near 0.8 hold the full
; 60 mm dead straight, which reads as wired in place. bend_stiffness then has
; to carry the curve past it: at 0.05 the body is string, so every degree of a
; 90 deg pull collects into one corner where the boot's taper ends and the
; minimum radius lands at 27 mm, under what an 8.3 mm jacket can take (4-6x OD).
; 0.2 holds it near 90 mm and spreads slack into a belly instead of a kinked
; loop. Do not push it past ~0.3: bend_stiffness sets the long-range constraint
; depth (1 + round(k*3)), and 0.6 costs +24% CPU per cable for a hose look.
;
; 1.80 m overall, the length a real composite lead shipped at, split so the
; breakout is part of it: a 1.65 m trunk (55 x 30 mm) plus a 150 mm tail per cord
; at the plug end (5 x 30 mm). The rest length is also the tether: every host
; clamps its plugs to trunk + tail from the attach point. Length is bought with
; segment COUNT, not a longer segment, so the boot and the minimum bend radius
; above stay where they were tuned in millimetres — but the solver is
; per-particle, so it costs about double (measured 0.56 -> 1.05 ms per cable per
; physics tick, 8 cables on a floor with collision on).
;
; THREE 3 mm wires, not one 8 mm cord: this is the lead that leaves a handheld
; when video out is switched on, and the real thing is a composite pigtail — a
; picture wire with the audio pair beside it. tube_radius is the wire's RADIUS,
; so 3 mm across is 0.0015. collision_radius has to cover the whole ribbon rather
; than one wire (3 x tube_radius, the ratio composite_cable.tscn uses).
;
; Frayed at the PLUG end only. The console end is a moulded pigtail — the three
; wires leave the shell inside one jacket and there is nothing there to take
; hold of — so the trunk anchors straight to the attach point and only the free
; end breaks out into its three connectors. fray_end_groups and the per-group
; anchors are wired in system.gd, which is where the plugs are known.
;
; No ribbon_colors: with the array absent every cord falls back to rope_color, so
; all three come out black — the colour code belongs to the connectors, not the
; wire (RcaJack.WIRE_BLACK, written as a literal because a .tscn cannot reference
; a constant). A model can still override count and colour per channel through
; its video-channel descriptor; the DS's bottom screen is one wire that way, and
; a one-wire channel keeps a plain unfrayed cord with its single plug on the end.
; See RetroSystemModel.get_video_channels.
[node name="VerletRope" type="VerletRope" parent="."]
segment_count = 55
segment_length = 0.03
constraint_iterations = 8
bend_stiffness = 0.2
tube_radius = 0.0015
tube_sides = 8
smoothing = 2
rope_color = Color(0.05, 0.05, 0.06, 1.0)
ribbon_count = 3
surface_collision_mask = 7
collision_radius = 0.0045
self_collision = true
end_align_stiffness = 0.7
end_stiffness = 0.45
end_stiff_segments = 2
fray_segments_end = 5
fray_taper_segments = 3
